.TH "MRG" "1" "Oct 2026" "Portuale @VERSION@" "Portuale"
.SH "NAME"
mrg \- Portuale's command\-line front end to the emerge code path
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B portuale mrg
[\fIemerge options\fR] [\fB\-\-vdb\-backend\fR=\fIBACKEND\fR]
[\fB\-\-vdb\-path\fR=\fIPATH\fR] [\fIatom\fR ...]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBmrg\fR accepts emerge's own options, with the same spellings, and runs
the same resolver and merge code as \fBemerge\fR(1).  Only the options
described here are specific to \fBmrg\fR; see \fBportuale mrg \-\-help\fR
for the full list, including the \fB\-\-remote\-*\fR options.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-vdb\-backend\fR=\fIfiles\fR|\fIsqlite\fR|\fIredb\fR
Where the installed\-package database (the VDB) lives.  \fBfiles\fR, the
default, is the historic \fIROOT\fR\fB/var/db/pkg\fR tree.  \fBsqlite\fR
reads one SQLite file.  \fBredb\fR is not implemented yet and is refused.
.TP
\fB\-\-vdb\-path\fR=\fIPATH\fR
The database file for \fBsqlite\fR.  The default is
\fIROOT\fR\fB/var/lib/portage/vdb.sqlite\fR.  It is ignored, with a
warning, when the backend is \fBfiles\fR.
When the sqlite database was converted from the files backend, a warning
is printed if the files VDB has changed since conversion; re-convert with
\fBportuale vdb convert --force\fR(1) to update the database.
.br
At startup, once, before any merge of the run, a warning
(\fB!!! \fR\fIcategory/pf\fR\fB was left half\-merged in\fR ...) is printed for each entry an
interrupted merge left in the \fBmerging\fR state in the database.  Nothing is removed
automatically; use \fBportuale vdb status\fR and \fBportuale vdb sweep\fR
(see \fBportuale\-vdb\fR(1)).  The \fBfiles\fR backend gives no such warning.
.SH "SELECTION"
The backend is chosen only when asked for; \fBmrg\fR never detects a
database on its own.  The first of these that is set wins:
.IP 1. 3
\fB\-\-vdb\-backend\fR
.IP 2. 3
\fBPORTUALE_VDB_BACKEND\fR in the process environment
.IP 3. 3
\fBPORTUALE_VDB_BACKEND\fR in \fBmake.conf\fR(5)
.IP 4. 3
\fBfiles\fR
.LP
The path follows the same order with \fB\-\-vdb\-path\fR and
\fBPORTUALE_VDB_PATH\fR, then the default above.
.LP
A database that does not exist is an error (exit status 1): \fBmrg\fR
never creates an empty one.  Make it first with
\fBportuale vdb convert \-\-from files:\fIROOT\fR \fB\-\-to sqlite:\fIPATH\fR
(see \fBportuale\-vdb\fR(1)).  A \fB\-\-pretend\fR run opens the database
read\-only.
.LP
The database serves the target \fBROOT\fR.  When \fBROOT\fR is not
\fB/\fR, the running root keeps its \fB/var/db/pkg\fR tree.  When the
backend is not \fBfiles\fR, \fBmrg\fR sets \fBPORTUALE_VDB_BACKEND\fR and
\fBPORTUALE_VDB_PATH\fR in the environment of the programs it starts.
.LP
With \fB\-\-remote\-hostname\fR, a \fBclient:\fR VDB
(\fB\-\-remote\-vdb\fR, the default) always stays a files tree, and
combining it with \fB\-\-vdb\-backend\fR or \fB\-\-vdb\-path\fR is an
error.
.LP
\fBemerge\fR has no such options and always uses the files tree.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR emerge (1),
.BR portuale\-vdb (1),
.BR make.conf (5)
